= Acknowledgements

Pursuing a PhD is choosing to embark on an adventure filled with uncertainties and unexpected turns.
The investment is considerable, but the journey and its rewards are just as significant.
Despite its solitary nature, one should not underestimate the people who support a candidate along the way.
I was fortunate to be surrounded by remarkable individuals who were instrumental throughout this journey.

I want to start by deeply thanking my three supervisors, Xavier Alameda-Pineda, Laurent Girin and Chris Reinke.
They trusted me from the very beginning by selecting me among many competitive applicants, and they accompanied me with unfaltering support until the very end.
My PhD certainly did not spare us scientific difficulties or setbacks, yet they always managed to keep the hope and vision alive when I sometimes felt like losing confidence.
Their complementary expertise, patience, and kindness made every discussion immensely valuable.
The hours we spent understanding problems, clarifying ideas, and exploring new directions helped me grow both scientifically and personally.
Xavi, Laurent, and Chris will remain models of dedication to me.

Besides my great supervisors, I had the honor of working with inspiring collaborators.
The RobotLearn team brings together young, talented, and driven individuals from diverse cultures, backgrounds, and experiences to tackle challenging research projects.
It was a blast to exchange ideas on specialized and novel subjects with such curious and brilliant minds.
Experiencing the rapid progress of AI together over these five years was deeply stimulating.
Our reading group sessions offered great opportunities to explore cutting-edge research.
Everyone was encouraged to ask questions and share their views, creating a supportive and enriching atmosphere.
I was grateful to share my successes, failures, and doubts with my teammates, who were always keen to offer their support.
Some of my fondest memories come from our daily meals at the _"Champ de l'Étoile"_ enjoying Patrick's desserts, our coffee breaks, and many excursions.

Xiaoyu Lin started her PhD at the same time as I did.
I have rarely known someone as exceptionally hard-working and dedicated.
I have the utmost respect for her discretion, kindness, and maturity.
She will undoubtedly have a successful and inspiring career that I will follow closely.
Andres, Louis, Alex, Anand, Kiru, Victor, and Lorenzo quickly became close friends, consistently cheering me up and making every shared moment enjoyable.
Radu, Soraya and Nicolas represent the roots and enduring spirit of the team, and hearing from their experience in research was always a pleasure.
I would also like to warmly thank the many other members of RobotLearn I met during these years, including Samir, Ahamed, Stéphane, Matthieu, Tim, Luis, Guillaume, Yihong, Xiaoyu Bie, Wen, Maxime, David, Ghazi, Zhiqi, Daniel, Natanaël, Alexandre and Saghar.
I must not forget Nathalie, our team assistant, without whom this bunch of nerds would definitely not get very far!

Now, let me briefly go back in time.
My academic path did not begin with this PhD, and the reason I was able to join such a talented team lies in the excellent professors I met and the diverse environments I experienced.
My years in the _classes préparatoires_ of _La Martinière Monplaisir_ gave me solid scientific foundations. There, I discovered the beauty of mathematics, physics, and engineering thanks to exceptionally dedicated professors such as Viviane Gaggioli, Laurence Lechuga, Cédric Gamelon, Xavier Pessoles, and Delphine Sembély, whose high expectations taught me rigor and hard work.
My time at Ensimag then opened my eyes to the world of computer science, mathematics, and artificial intelligence, surrounded by like-minded students eager to explore intricate topics.
Looking back, I am deeply grateful for the ten fulfilling years spent in such inspiring academic environments, culminating in this PhD.

I am grateful for the fantastic friends with whom I shared so many warm and carefree moments.
Our dinners, long discussions, hikes, and board games were a wonderful escape from the daily routine.
Lucas, Isabelle, Benjamin, and Thomas Girerd in Lyon, as well as Olivier Lim, Thomas Bauvent and Michael from Grenoble — thank you for all these moments.
Last but not least, my fellow flatmates Antoine, Olivier Brun, and Yangtao shared the PhD journey with me day to day.
Living with these cheerful guys and sharing our victories and doubts made this period lighter and more enjoyable.

I have practiced rock climbing for many years, and Grenoble offered an exceptional environment to progress.
Beyond its physical and mental benefits, climbing was essential to my well-being, helping me disconnect from research and surround myself with wonderful people.
The Grenoble climbing community is full of talented and welcoming climbers, and I met numerous partners over the past five years.
Rémy, Marjorie, Simon, Basile, Nicolas, Lionel, Thomas, Hugo, Gabin, Noé, Lilou, Coraline, Merlin, Manu, and all the other _Requins du CSU_ were incredible people to train and travel with.
Our trips to Chamonix, Fontainebleau, Céüse, and especially Ailefroide remain unforgettable.
The more experienced _adeptes de la force droite_, including Nico, Théo, Roman, and Mathieu, were strong, dedicated athletes I continue to look up to.
I also owe a lot to my coach, Adrien Pirolo, whose support and trust pushed me far beyond what I thought possible.
His guidance helped me grow steadily and confidently.
Let’s say he was my fourth supervisor.

My parents, Anne-Catherine and Francis, as well as my brother Tanguy, have provided me with unconditional and loving support.
They were always by my side during the difficult periods of this adventure, reminding me that life is not only about academic success and complex equations.
I was exceptionally lucky to have them around, and I truly realized how crucial a loving family is.
They deserve a large share of this success, as I would probably have given up without them.

I would like to express my sincere gratitude to the jury members, Prof. Didier Schwab, Prof. Elisa Ricci, Prof. Patrick Danès, and Dr. Romain Serizel, who agreed to review my work and participate in my defense.
Their insightful and challenging questions made the discussion both stimulating and enriching.
Patrick and Didier went through a careful and thorough review of my manuscript and provided thoughtful and precise feedback.

Last but not least, I want to thank all the people who attended my defense.
I was delighted to be surrounded by so many friendly faces on such an important day.
Sharing this success with all of them was the best celebration I could have hoped for.
